{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>all who go forth to the army in Israel</b> — This indicates that none goes forth to the army younger than twenty years. \"Lift up the head of all the congregation of the children of Israel\" — as they say to the tax-collector (לקוסטינר): lift the head of so-and-so; this is the language of Rashi. And it is possible that the reason for this is that one is not strong for warfare under twenty, as they said (Avot 5:21): at twenty, to pursue. But it is also possible that the meaning of <b>all who go forth to the army</b> is: all who go forth to be assembled in the congregation, for young men are not assembled among the people, for any gathering of a people is called \"צָבָא\" (army/host). And thus \"to perform the service of the service in the tent of meeting\" (Numbers 8:24), \"he shall return from the army of the service\" (ibid. verse 25), \"at the entrance of the tent of meeting, with the women who assembled\" (Exodus 38:8), and likewise \"the host of heaven\" (I Kings 22:19), \"and all their host I have commanded\" (Isaiah 45:12). And therefore regarding men of war it specifies \"from the army of the battle\" (Numbers 31:14), \"and they were enrolled by their genealogies in the army for battle\" (I Chronicles 7:40). And here it says <b>all who go forth to the army</b> in the manner of \"all who go out of the gate of his city\" (Genesis 34:24). And it says \"by their armies\" (לצבאתם) — meaning that they are many armies, for each and every tribe is a great army. And the expression that Rashi wrote: as they say to the tax-collector (לקוסטינר), lift the head of so-and-so — it has not been clarified to me why they expounded it in a negative sense. If it is because they died in the wilderness, and regarding the tribe of Levi it says \"פְּקֹד\" ( Numbers 3:15) because they were not included in the decree — but behold, in the second counting of those who entered the land, the same is said: \"Lift up the head of all the congregation of the children of Israel\" (Numbers 26:2)! But in the homily of Vayikra Rabbah, they expound it for praise: \"שְׂאוּ\" is nothing but an expression of greatness, as it is written \"Pharaoh will lift up your head and restore you to your station\" (Genesis 40:13). The Holy One, blessed be He, said to Israel: I have given you preeminence and I have likened you to Myself — just as I have preeminence over all who come into the world, as it is said (I Chronicles 29:11) \"Yours, YHWH, is the kingdom, and You are exalted as head over all,\" so too I have made you preeminent, as it is said \"Lift up the head of all the congregation of the children of Israel,\" to fulfill what is said \"and He has raised a horn for His people\" (Psalms 148:14), and likewise it says \"and HaShem your Elohim will set you supreme above all the nations of the earth\" (Deuteronomy 28:1). And furthermore, I found in Midbar Sinai Rabbah (Numbers Rabbah 1:9) where they said: Rabbi Pinchas said in the name of Rabbi Idi: What is written at the beginning of the book, \"Lift up the head\"? Exalt the head — it does not say magnify the head, but rather \"Lift up the head,\" like a person who says to the tax-collector: \"Lift the head of so-and-so (סַב רֵישֵׁיהּ דִּפְלָן).\" Here a hint was given to Moses — \"Lift up the head\" — meaning that if they merit, they will ascend to greatness, as it is written \"Pharaoh will lift up your head and restore you to your station,\" and if they do not merit, they will all die, as it is written \"Pharaoh will lift up your head from upon you and hang you on a tree\" (Genesis 40:19). And so the expression, according to the intent, is interpreted for good for the good ones, and since it is an expression of greatness and was said in the first counting, so too it was said in the second counting." "<b>you shall count them</b> — the meaning of \"פְּקִידָה\" (pekudah) is remembrance and oversight of a matter, as in the language of \"and YHWH remembered Sarah as He had said\" (Genesis 21:1), and this is its meaning in every place; none is excluded, in my opinion. And likewise \"פִּקָּדוֹן\" (pledge/deposit), because its safekeeping and oversight are upon it. And when He commanded to count Israel, He says <b>you shall count them</b> to hint that they should not count them directly, but rather that they should give the ransom of their souls, a half-shekel, and through them He will oversee and know the number of the people. And it says regarding David \"the number of those counted of the people\" (II Samuel 24:9), for he knew their number through the counting of the ransom, for it is far from me that David would not be scrupulous regarding what the verse says \"and there shall be no plague among them when you count them\" (Exodus 30:12). And if you say that David erred, why did Joab not make shekels, and the king's matter was detestable to him, and he said to him \"Why does my lord seek this thing? Why should he be a cause of guilt to Israel?\" (I Chronicles 21:3), and why did he not count them with shekels so as not to sin? But in my opinion, the wrath was upon him because he counted them for no need, for he was not going forth to war and was doing nothing with them at that time, but only to gladden his heart that he ruled over a great people. And this is the statement of Joab (II Samuel 24:3): \"May YHWH your Elohim add to the people a hundredfold as many as they are... and my lord the king, why does he desire this thing?\" And I saw in Midbar Sinai Rabbah (Numbers Rabbah 2:17): Rabbi Eliezer said in the name of Rabbi Yosi ben Zimra: All the time Israel was counted for a need — they did not fall short; when not for a need — they fell short. When were they counted for a need? In the days of Moses, and with the banners, and in the division of the land. When not for a need — in the days of David. And it is further possible to explain and say that David commanded to count every man of Israel — that is, from twenty years old and upward, for in his counting \"from twenty years old and upward\" is not mentioned, but rather he said \"Go, count Israel... and I will know their number\" (I Chronicles 21:2). And this was his punishment, for the verse did not permit counting except from twenty years old and upward with shekels. And because this is not explicit in the verse, he erred, for he thought that what it says \"and there shall be no plague among them\" refers to the shekels which serve as their ransom. But Joab paid attention and was concerned about the matter. And the verse stirs my heart in this regard by what it says: \"And David did not take their number from twenty years old and downward, for YHWH had said to multiply Israel as the stars of heaven. Joab son of Zeruiah began to count but did not finish, and there came wrath upon Israel on account of this\" (I Chronicles 27:23–24). It would appear that Joab's counting included those below twenty, and that was the wrath — that YHWH does not desire all of Israel to be subject to counting, for He multiplies them as the stars of heaven, as He said \"if you can count them\" (Genesis 15:5). And the verse that says about that counting \"and all Israel were a thousand thousand and a hundred thousand men drawing sword\" (I Chronicles 21:5) — its meaning is not that all of them were those who go forth to the army in Israel, but rather its explanation is that they were hale and strong for battle. It tells us that he did not count the sick and the weak and the elderly, because the king's matter was detestable to him. And this is a very fitting interpretation. And according to the opinion of the midrashic homily (Berakhot 62b) that there was an actual counting, they all erred, and therefore it says (II Samuel 24:1) \"Go, count Israel,\" and it is written (ibid. verse 10) \"afterward he counted the people,\" for in the Torah no counting (סְפִירָה) is mentioned regarding them at all, for the meaning of <b>by the number of names</b> is that each one declares his name when counted through the ransom."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {0 "I have translated לקוסטינר as \"tax-collector\" based on the context of the Aramaic phrases cited.", 1 "I have rendered ספירה as \"counting\" (direct head-count) distinct from פקידה, which Ramban glosses as oversight/remembrance via the half-shekel ransom."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:02:51.911780722Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}